script:
{
  "topic": "The hidden world of secret shoppers and professional refunders",
  "title": "POV: You're a Secret Shopper and You Find a Darker Way to Get Paid",
  "sections": [
    {
      "name": "Level 1: The Catalyst",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person POV of hands scrolling through job listings on a laptop. The screen glows on the face of a tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie. The room is dark, lit only by the monitor.",
          "narration": "The promises come at you in the dark. 'Make Your Own Hours'. 'Get Paid to Shop'. You've been scrolling for two hours, and the nonsense job listings are starting to blur together. But this one, 'Mystery Shopper', sticks. It feelsâ¦ easy. A way to make a few extra bucks without another soul-crushing interview.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Extreme close-up on a mouse clicking the 'Apply Now' button. The click sounds loud and definitive in the quiet room.",
          "narration": "You click. It's not a decision, really. It's an impulse. A tiny bet against the universe. The application is simple. Name, address, a few questions about your shopping habits. Two hours of online training videos follow, full of corporate jargon about 'brand integrity' and 'customer experience metrics'. You half-listen while playing a game on your phone.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A crisp, new check is deposited into an ATM. First-person POV, holding the check. The numbers are clear: $1,950.",
          "narration": "Two days later, a check arrives via courier. $1,950. Your heart jumps. It feels like a lottery ticket just paid out. The email instructions are specific: deposit the check immediately. This is your first assignment. The money is for your 'purchases and compensation'. The speed of it all feels like validation. You were right to bet on this.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 2: The Descent",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: INSTRUCTIONS: Buy five $300 gift cards from Store X. Keep $350 as your fee. Wire the rest.",
          "narration": "The instructions are your first red flag, but you ignore it. You're supposed to buy gift cards from a specific electronics store. Five of them, $300 each. You're told to keep $350 for yourself and wire the PINs from the back of the cards to your 'handler'. The task is to report on the checkout clerk's friendliness. It seems weirdly simple for a $350 payout.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot from inside a car, looking at a bank entrance. A phone is in your hand, showing a banking app with a bright red notification: 'CHECK RETURNED: INSUFFICIENT FUNDS'.",
          "narration": "The feeling lasts for exactly three days. Then, the notification arrives. The check bounced. The $1,950 that felt so real has vanished from your account. But the $1,500 you spent on gift cards? That money is very real. And it's gone. Your bank account, already low, is now negative. -$1,588.24, to be precise, after the overdraft fees.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The same tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie is now sitting at a cheap kitchen table, head in his hands, surrounded by printed-out bank statements.",
          "narration": "You've been had. It's a classic fake check scam. You, who prided yourself on being smart, on seeing the angles, fell for the oldest trick in the book. The shame is worse than the debt. You report the company, but they've vanished. The website is gone. The emails bounce. You're left holding the bag. A bag that costs you two months' rent.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close-up on a web browser typing 'legitimate mystery shopper forums' into a search bar. The search results are a long list of blue links.",
          "narration": "The anger pushes you back online. Not for jobs, but for answers. You dig for real platforms, legitimate ones. You find them, but they're different. The pay is $12 for a fast-food audit, $25 to time a tire rotation, plus reimbursement for an oil change. It's not the windfall you imagined. It's grinding, meticulous work. But you need the money. So you sign up. For real, this time.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 3: The Unspoken Rules",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person view of hands meticulously filling out a digital report on a tablet. The form has dozens of tiny checkboxes and fields for specific timings, names, and details.",
          "narration": "You learn the first rule of the real game: Everything is measured. You don't just buy a coffee. You time the transaction to the second. You note if the barista said your name. You count the number of napkins in the dispenser. You submit a 40-question report with photo evidence for a profit of $8 and a free latte. It's exhausting.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A spreadsheet on a computer screen. Rows and rows of small numbers. 'Assignment Fee: $15'. 'Reimbursement: $45.90'. 'Net Profit: $15'. The total at the bottom reads: '$212 for the month'.",
          "narration": "You learn the second rule: The math is brutal. After two months of non-stop assignments, you've made $478. You've spent hours driving, documenting, and uploading. When you factor in gas and the time spent, you're making less than minimum wage. The promise of 'making a living' feels like a distant lie told by someone else.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: On a legitimate mystery shopper forum, you see a private message from a user named 'ShopCop'. The message is friendly: 'Hey, saw you're new. Watch out for the small print on reports.'",
          "narration": "You make a friend, or something like it, on the legit forums. An older user named ShopCop. He tells you to be careful. 'Rule three,' he writes, 'the companies that hire you are always looking for a reason not to pay you.' He tells you he once lost a $150 reimbursement for a steak dinner because his photo of the receipt was slightly blurry.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: An email on your screen shows a rejected report. The reason: 'Timestamp on photo does not match timestamp on receipt by 47 seconds.' Your reimbursement of $75 is denied.",
          "narration": "You learn ShopCop's lesson the hard way. A grocery audit, $75 worth of food, denied. Why? The photo of your car in the parking lot was taken 47 seconds before the timestamp on your receipt. You followed the rules, but you broke an unwritten one. The frustration is immense. You have the groceries, but you're out the money.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",n          "visual": "broll: POV looking at a grimy, flickering laptop screen in a dark room. The screen shows a forum with a dark theme, username 'ByteGhost44'. The text is dense and full of slang.",
          "narration": "That $75 pushes you over the edge. You remember a Reddit thread that mentioned other forums, places where the real 'pros' talk. Names like Nulled and Sinisterly. You create a throwaway account. The language is different here. It's not about 'customer experience'. It's about 'SE'ing' a company.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: Forum Post: 'SE'd a new 7-series TV. Claimed DNA. Easy win. Flair updated.'",
          "narration": "Social Engineering. That's what 'SE' means. You see terms you don't understand. 'DNA' - Did Not Arrive. 'Boxing' - sending back an empty box. 'Wardrobing' - buying an outfit, wearing it for a weekend, and returning it on Monday. They have flair next to their usernames, little icons for their biggest scores. It's a status symbol.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close up on your character's eyes, wide with a mix of fear and intrigue as they scroll through the forum posts. The screen's glow reflects in their pupils.",
          "narration": "You learn the fourth rule, the one from this shadow world: The customer is always right, especially when they're lying. These people have turned customer service policies into a weapon. They have repositories of scripts, guides on how to fake frustration, even audio files of babies crying to use in the background during phone calls.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: You watch a screen-recorded tutorial video from the forum. It shows someone using basic photo editing software to add a crack to a picture of a brand new TV screen.",
          "narration": "You find a tutorial for faking a damaged item. The user calmly explains how to take a picture of your brand new television, then use a simple app to draw a convincing-looking crack on the screen. 'Make sure the reflection in the screen matches the room you're in,' he advises. 'Details matter.' It's a masterclass in deception.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV of hands hovering over a keyboard, typing out a question on the dark-themed forum. 'How do you start with a DNA claim?' The cursor blinks.",
          "narration": "You read stories of people getting $1,000 laptops, 85-inch TVs, designer clothes, all for nothing. They post their 'wins' like trophies. A part of you is disgusted. But another part, the part that remembers the -$1,588.24 and the denied $75 report, is taking notes. The risk is huge, but the math is suddenly very, very different.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A shopping cart icon on a screen fills up. A high-end graphics card, a motherboard, a CPU. The total at the bottom reads: $2,450.",
          "narration": "You pick your target. A big box electronics retailer known for its massive volume and overseas call centers. You load a cart with high-end computer parts, the kind of things you could never afford. Your heart is pounding so hard you can feel it in your jaw. You're just looking. That's what you tell yourself.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie stares at the 'Confirm Purchase' button. A bead of sweat drips down his temple.",
          "narration": "But then your finger moves. The mouse clicks. The order is confirmed. Your card is charged $2,450. For a moment, there's only panic. You just pushed all your chips into the center of the table. There is no folding now. You've just entered the game.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 4: The Bottom & The Pivot",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV staring at a tracking number on a shipping website. The status reads 'Delivered'. The package is sitting on your doorstep in the photo.",
          "narration": "The boxes arrive two days later. The tracking number updates to 'Delivered'. This is the moment of truth. According to the forums, you wait 48 hours. Let the system register the delivery. Let the digital ink dry. It is the longest 48 hours of your life. Every noise outside sounds like police.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person view of hands dialing a customer service number on a smartphone. The contact name is the electronics company.",
          "narration": "You follow the script you found online. You lower your voice, try to sound frustrated but not hysterical. 'I'm calling about order number 74B-291X. It says it was delivered, but I've got nothing here. I've been home all day.' You feel like you're reading lines in a school play.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: An extreme close-up of an ear, with an earbud in, listening intently. The background is blurred, focusing on the act of listening.",
          "narration": "The agent on the other end is polite, distant. He asks you to check with your neighbors. To look behind the bushes. You play along, putting the phone down for a minute, then coming back. 'Nothing.' you say, your voice tight. He puts you on hold. The hold music is a cheerful, looping synth track that feels like a mockery.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A customer service chat window on a screen. A message from the agent reads: 'I have processed a full refund for you. It should appear in your account in 3-5 business days.'",
          "narration": "He comes back on the line. 'Okay, sir. I've put in for a full refund. We're very sorry for the inconvenience.' And that's it. It's over. No argument. No investigation. It's so anticlimactic you almost feel disappointed. You hang up the phone and your hands are shaking.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: Bank Account: Pending Deposit +$2,450.00.",
          "narration": "Four days later, the money is back in your account. The computer components are sitting on your living room floor. You got them for free. The pivot happens right there. The slow, grinding work of legitimate secret shopping is over. You've found a backdoor in the system, and you just walked right through it.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tense scene in a dimly lit parking lot at dusk. POV of you handing a box of computer parts to a stranger whose face is obscured by a hoodie.",
          "narration": "You learn the next rule: Don't keep the goods, flip them. You arrange to sell the parts to a guy from an online marketplace. The meeting is tense. He counts out the cash, you hand over the unopened boxes. He doesn't ask questions, and you don't offer answers. The $1,960 in cash feels heavier than it should.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The man in the grey hoodie is at his laptop again, but this time his posture is confident. He's browsing a high-end clothing website.",
          "narration": "The fear is replaced by a different feeling: control. You do it again. A designer jacket, $800. This time you try the 'Empty Box' method. You mail back the original packaging, but empty. You get the refund notification before the box even arrives at their warehouse. It feels like a superpower.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: An online order for an espresso machine. The status shows 'Back-ordered. Expected ship date: 3 weeks.' A look of panic crosses your face.",
          "narration": "You get cocky. You order a $1,200 espresso machine, but it's back-ordered. The refund comes through instantly, but you don't have the product. For three weeks, you live in a state of anxiety. What if they realize their mistake and cancel the shipment? You can't flip what you don't have. It's a new kind of stress, managing phantom assets.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A pile of cash being counted on a table. Mostly hundreds and fifties. It's a significant amount.",
          "narration": "But the machine arrives. You flip it for $900 cash. The money is piling up. You're not just a shopper anymore. You're a tester. A tester of systems, of policies, of human indifference. You are exploiting the very thing you were once paid to measure. The irony is not lost on you, but it's hard to feel bad about it.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close-up on a message board. A new post is titled 'Warning: Company X is cracking down.' The post describes a new policy requiring a police report for certain claims.",
          "narration": "Then, a post appears on the forum. The electronics company you first hit has changed its policy. They're requiring police reports for all DNA claims over $500. Someone got sloppy, hit them too many times from the same area. The game has changed. The easy wins are drying up.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 5: The Escalation",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Scrolling through the forum, you find a new section you never noticed before. It's labeled 'Services' with a lock icon next to it. You just gained access.",
          "narration": "Your success on the forum has granted you access to a private, members-only section. 'Services'. It's not about doing it yourself. It's about hiring someone to do it for you. This is the big league. 'Refund as a Service' - RaaS.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: Service Ad: 'SPECTRE | Pro Refunder | 15% Fee | Any Store, Any Value | Guaranteed or No Charge.'",
          "narration": "The ads are brazen. Professional refunders, offering their services for a fee. Usually 10 to 30 percent. They talk about having 'insiders' at shipping companies and using 'advanced social engineering methods'. One name stands out, with a reputation for hitting impossible targets: Spectre.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person POV of a chat window on an encrypted messaging app like Telegram. The profile picture of the contact is an anonymous anime character.",
          "narration": "You're hesitant. This feels different. It's one thing to lie on the phone yourself. It's another to bring in a total stranger, a professional criminal. But the allure of hitting bigger targets is too strong. You contact Spectre. The instructions are simple: give him the order number and your account login.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": true
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie is pacing nervously in his apartment, constantly checking his phone. He looks more stressed than ever.",
          "narration": "This is the twist. You're no longer the operator. You're the client. You've outsourced the crime. You place an order for a top-of-the-line camera. $4,500. You send the details to Spectre. And then you wait. All you have is the word of an anonymous avatar on a hacker forum. You've given him your name, address, and account passwords.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A screenshot of a customer service chat log. The text is sophisticated, using legal jargon and internal policy codes. It's clearly a professional at work.",
          "narration": "Spectre sends you a transcript of his chat. It's masterful. He doesn't just claim it didn't arrive. He references shipping lane discrepancies, quotes their own terms of service, and uses AI-generated images of a damaged shipping box that look utterly real. This is a level you couldn't even imagine.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A new chat message from Spectre: 'Initial approach failed. They wanted a police report. Had to pivot to a different method. Don't worry, I handled it.'",
          "narration": "Halfway through, you get a message: 'They're asking for a police report.' Your stomach drops. But Spectre is unfazed. 'Pivoting,' he says. An hour later, he messages again. 'Done. I convinced them the item was stolen by their own delivery driver. They're issuing the refund to avoid an internal investigation.' He turned their security against them.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: Email Notification: 'Your refund of $4,500 has been processed.' Followed by a crypto payment for $675.",
          "narration": "The refund is approved. You're stunned. Spectre sends a crypto wallet address. You send him his fee: $675, 15%. The camera is yours. The profit, after the fee, is $3,825. You didn't even have to make a phone call. The efficiency is addictive.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A rapid montage of expensive items being unboxed: a luxury watch, a drone, a VR headset. Then, you're seen pulling up to your apartment in a slightly newer, used car.",
          "narration": "You have a system. Order, send details, get refund, flip item. The money piles up. Ten thousand. Twenty. Fifty. You pay off your debts. You buy a better car. The numbers on your banking app, once a source of dread, are now a high score. You are living a life you couldn't afford just months ago.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The man, no longer in a grey hoodie but a designer jacket, looks over his shoulder on a busy street, looking paranoid.",
          "narration": "But with the money comes a new kind of fear. You're not just a lone wolf. You're tied to someone else. Someone who knows what you're doing. Someone who has a list of your transactions. You bought convenience, but the price was leverage. You are no longer your own boss.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A new message from 'Spectre' on the encrypted app. 'Time for the next one, my friend. Don't want to stop now, do we? We have a good thing going.'",
          "narration": "You think about stopping. Cashing out. But then a message from Spectre arrives. 'Ready for the next one?' It's not really a question. You're part of his business model now. His reliable income stream. And he's not going to let that go easily. You've escaped the 9-to-5 grind, only to find yourself with a new, more dangerous boss.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 6: The Reversal",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A close-up on a message from 'Spectre': 'My fee is now 25%. My methods have gotten more complex. You understand.'",
          "narration": "The rules change. Spectre's fee goes from 15% to 25%. He says the stores are getting smarter, so his work is harder. You know he's squeezing you, but what can you do? Argue? You're his accomplice. You have no negotiating power. You pay it, the cost of doing business.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV staring at a computer screen showing a news article headline: 'Major Retailer Partners with Feds to Crack Down on Billions in Return Fraud.'",
          "narration": "The world starts to feel smaller. News articles appear about retailers using advanced analytics and AI to flag suspicious accounts. They're building profiles. Linking names, addresses, and payment methods. The game of cat and mouse is escalating into an arms race.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The man is anxiously looking out his window, peeking through the blinds at a normal-looking delivery truck. He looks exhausted.",
          "narration": "Paranoia becomes your new roommate. Every delivery truck on your street could be surveillance. Every friendly email from a retailer feels like a trap. You start using different addresses, P.O. boxes, friend's houses. The logistics become a nightmare. You're a ghost, haunted by a trail of tracking numbers.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: You're on a forum, looking at another refunder's profile. A huge red banner is stamped across it: 'BANNED - FBI INFORMANT'.",
          "narration": "You briefly consider finding another service, someone cheaper than Spectre. But then you see a post. A rival refunder has been exposed as an FBI informant. His clients are being arrested one by one. The forum moderators ban him, but the damage is done. You realize that Spectre, for all his menace, has kept you safe. You're stuck with him.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A phone call screen shows an incoming call from 'Mom'. You silence it, looking stressed and preoccupied.",
          "narration": "Your real life starts to fray. You cancel plans with friends because you're waiting on a package you can't have delivered to your own home. You ignore a call from your mom because you're in the middle of a tense crypto transfer to Spectre. The isolation you felt when you were broke is nothing compared to the isolation you feel now.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A message from 'Spectre' contains a photo of your apartment building, taken from the street. The text reads: 'Just making sure the address is correct for the next shipment.'",
          "narration": "Then comes the message that freezes your blood. It's from Spectre. A photo of your apartment building. 'Just checking on a delivery,' it says. But you haven't ordered anything to your own address in months. He's just reminding you. He knows where you sleep. The reversal is complete. You are not in control.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A new message from Spectre: 'I was looking you up. Say hello to your mother in Ohio for me.' Your face turns pale.",
          "narration": "The next message is worse. 'Say hello to your mother in Ohio for me.' You never told him where your mother lives. He researched you. He knows your family. The threat is no longer about money or exposure to the police. It's personal. It's physical. The line has been crossed, and he crossed it intentionally.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: Legal Warning: Wire Fraud - Up to 30 years imprisonment. $1,000,000 fine.",
          "narration": "You finally do the research you should have done at the beginning. You look up the actual penalties. It's not a slap on the wrist. It's federal wire fraud. The numbers are terrifying. Up to 30 years in prison. A million-dollar fine. You haven't made a million dollars. Not even close. You've risked decades of your life for a used car and some designer clothes.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A hand hovering over a 'Delete Account' button on the encrypted messaging app. The hand is trembling.",
          "narration": "You decide to get out. You tell Spectre you're done. He replies almost instantly. 'No, you're not.' He sends you a file. It's a spreadsheet. Every order, every date, every tracking number, every address you've ever used. 'It would be a shame if this got sent to the wrong people,' he writes. 'Let's do one more. A big one.'",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The man sits in his dark apartment, the glow of the monitor on his face. He looks gaunt, terrified. He's clicking 'Confirm Purchase' on a $22,000 diamond necklace.",
          "narration": "He has a target. A high-end jeweler. An item worth $22,000. This is a league you never wanted to be in. The risk is astronomical. But the threat is real. He has everything he needs to burn you to the ground. You're not even thinking about the money anymore. You're just trying to survive. Trying to buy your way out of the hole you dug for yourself.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 7: The New Reality",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tense, silent chat window. You've sent the order details. Spectre has been offline for 12 hours. The 'last seen' status is a constant source of anxiety.",
          "narration": "The refund doesn't come in an hour. Or a day. Spectre is silent. The $22,000 charge sits on your credit card like a time bomb. You message him. No reply. You message again. Nothing. The silence is louder than any threat he ever made.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A news article on a phone screen. 'International Cybercrime Ring Busted. Dozens Arrested in 'Refund as a Service' Sting Operation.'",
          "narration": "Three days later, you see a news headline. An international bust. Dozens of arrests in a 'Refund as a Service' sting. The article mentions the forums by name. Nulled. Sinisterly. You feel a strange mix of terror and relief. Spectre is gone. The spreadsheet is probably on an FBI server now. But the necklace is still on its way to your apartment.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
